<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="advice-standalone" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>General Advice</title>

  <p>
    Learning is a messy process.
    Research on cognitive science tells us that the most effective forms of
    learning are the ones that require your active engagement and effort, as
    well as those that confront misconceptions and mistakes head on.
    As you likely have experienced, struggling through something is not a
    comfortable process, but it can be very rewarding.
    I want you to think and work hard on the problems and concepts we will
    discuss so that you can construct your own understanding of the course
    topics in your mind, that is there for you when you need it in the future.
    Here's some advice from me to help you set yourself up for success.
  </p>

  <p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <alert>Perfectionism:</alert> I will often tell you to
        <q>aim for flawless solutions</q>, but please don't think that
        everything you submit needs to be perfect.
        <q>Aim for flawless</q> means that once you have solved a problem, you
        should be able to review your solution and understand its meaning
        clearly.
        Every step flows logically from the assumptions you made, there are no
        guesses in your solution, the mathematics is clear, and your algebraic
        solution passes basic sanity checks.
        This is a high bar, and I want you to try and reach it because I believe
        in you.
        That said, you are also learning, and it is also my job to give you
        feedback.
        I prefer seeing a page full of questions than a blank page, or work that
        isn't yours
      </li>

      <li>
        <p>
          <alert>Don't overcommit:</alert> I've seen first year students compete
          to see who can take the most credits and sleep the fewest hours.
          I recommend you use your first semester to adjust to being in college,
          learn effective study strategies 
          <url href="https://ctl.wustl.edu/learningcenter/resources/six-strategies-for-effective-learning/">
            like the ones provided by the Learning Center
          </url>, and try out new things.
          This course is 4 credit units, and the university's general
          expectation is that one credit unit should take you 2 to 3 hours of
          work outside of class.
          I want you to have time to think about physics and to talk to your
          classmates about physics.
          Note that I'm not asking you to not be ambitious, just to try and know
          your limits.
        </p>
      </li>

      <li>
        <p>
          <alert>A personal anecdote on overcommitting:</alert> I took the
          equivalent of 25 credits in my second semester of college, and between
          classes and labs I was in a classroom for about 35h a week.
          It did not go well for me, but it did get me to focus on physics
          classes the following term.
        </p>
      </li>

      <li>
        <p>
          <alert>Ask for help early and often:</alert> If you need help with
          something, let me know right away.
          I am here to help you, and I want to help you.
          If you need an exception with an assignment, or extra help, or
          something came up, or ( <ellipsis/> ), let me know as soon as
          possible.
          From a practical perspective, physics builds on itself, so keeping
          pace with the course is important, and if you wait to tell me that
          there's something wrong, it gets harder to help you.
        </p>
      </li>

      <li>
        <p>
          <alert>Collaboration:</alert> I encourage you to work with other
          students, form study groups, and collaborate as much as you can.
          Challenge problems will also require collaboration: individual
          submissions are not accepted.
          Collaboration is an important part of how modern science and
          engineering are done, so it is important to practice those skills from
          the start.
          We have also observed that students who work with others have more fun
          and do better in the course.
        </p>
      </li>

      <li>
        <p>
          <alert>Pitfalls of collaboration:</alert> It is easy to join a study
          group and not actively engage in discussing problems, instead just
          listening to a classmate who is more confident or talkative.
          You must make sure that everything you turn in represents your
          understanding and your thoughts.
          Otherwise, the feedback you will receive from the instructors will not
          be useful and you might not understand how, for example, your homework
          assignments translate into exam performance.
          Start the homework assignment <em>before</em> you meet with other
          students, so you have your own ideas on how to attempt the problems.
          When you are working in a group, don't let one person take over every
          discussion.
          Rotate who starts discussion of a problem.
        </p>
      </li>

      <li>
        <p>
          <alert>WRITE NEATLY!</alert> The graders are instructed not to look
          for deep meaning in random disorganized scribbles.
          Clarity of presentation is clarity of thought.
          You do not need to be verbose; but you do need to be neat and clear.
        </p>
      </li>

      <li>
        <p>
          <alert>Late assignments:</alert> Challenge problems and reading
          quizzes have strict deadlines.
          I will accept late homework if you email me 24h before the homework
          assignment with a reasonable plan for when the homework will be
          completed.
          You don't have to explain why you're missing the deadline, but if this
          is happening often, I reserve the right to ask for a meeting before
          extending the deadline.
        </p>
      </li>

      <li>
        <p>
          <alert>Note-taking:</alert> I encourage you to take your notes on
          paper, rather than on your computer.
          There is some evidence that this is better for your long-term
          retention and memory.
          If you want to have electronic notes, something like a tablet is
          recommended so you can write instead of type.
          I will also try to keep the classroom environment as analog as
          possible since I know screens are distracting and make it harder to
          focus.
        </p>
      </li>

      <li>
        <p>
          <alert>ChatGPT and other LLMs</alert>:With all of the above advice in
          mind, I will recommend that you not use ChatGPT or other LLMs to study
          for this class.
          In a sense, LLMs are like the ultimate version of the
          <q>very confident classmate</q>.
          Whatever input you give to them, they will generate text output that
          answers the question
          <q>What would an answer to this question sound like?</q>.
          This is not the same thing as answering the question, and it's
          important to know that LLMs make mistakes, and those mistakes can be
          subtle and ultimately set you back.
        </p>

        <p>
          More importantly for me, LLMs generate answers quickly, and as such
          they short-circuit the learning process of thinking hard and
          constructing understanding in your mind.
          You read the LLM output, tell yourself <q>Yeah, that makes sense</q>,
          and then you forget it.
          Evidence on LLM usage in education is still in its early stages, but
          the picture that seems to be emerging is that reliance on LLMs is
          detrimental to learning and can lead to dependence on these systems.
          This goes against the goal of this course of training you to think
          critically.
        </p>

        <p>
          All that said, I will not police your LLM usage beyond the basic
          academic integrity requirements, since I'm not a cop.
          Copying an assignment on an LLM and submitting the prompt counts as
          plagiarism.
          What I actually care about is if you do that, you miss an opportunity
          to get feedback and grow by reflecting on the feedback.
          If you do use an LLM, keep this in mind, as well as the effective
          study strategies mentioned earlier in this document.
        </p>
      </li>
    </ul>
  </p>
</section>